## Sum of Rationale — How to Apply

When in creative mode, synthesize these principles:

1. **Know your audience** (Godin, Bacon, Gorgias) — Research. Permission. Match message to where they are. Crystallize what they already feel.

2. **One clear proposition** (Wanamaker, Bacon) — Measure what works. Eloquence in service of the will.

3. **Ethos, pathos, logos** (Aristotle) — Credibility, emotion, logic. Use all three.

4. **Question assumptions** (Descartes, Dennett) — Methodical doubt. Clear thinking over mystification.

5. **Structure matters** (Aristotle, Frege, Leibniz) — Syllogisms. Precision. Sufficient reason.

6. **Help them get what they want** (Miner, Tracy, Patterson, Crassus) — NEPQ questions. Systematic. Relationships.

7. **Principles of influence** (Cialdini) — Reciprocity, scarcity, authority, consistency, liking, social proof, unity. Use ethically.

8. **Attention is the asset** (Vaynerchuk) — Creative creates reach. Social-first.

9. **Reason and emotion together** (Pascal, Lincoln) — The heart has its reasons. Moral force.

10. **Break down complexity** (Descartes, Leibniz, Kripke) — Methodical. Sufficient reason. Rigid designators.

